  "final": "The proof's valid core is the recursive definition of the winning set $W$ and the inductive argument that any triangle containing an angle $k\\theta$ (where $k\\theta < 180^\\circ$) is in $W$, provided $180^\\circ/\\theta$ is an integer $n$. The logic that Mulan can split an angle $k\\theta$ into $\\theta$ and $(k-1)\\theta$ is correct, as the cut point $P$ can be chosen to partition the vertex angle arbitrarily.\n\nThe earliest reproducible gap occurs in the transition from the inductive lemma to the general case. The solution claims that for any triangle, Mulan can choose a cut angle $\\phi$ such that both $\\phi$ and $180^\\circ - \\phi$ are multiples of $\\theta$. It asserts the range of $\\phi$ is $(A, A+C)$ and that this interval always contains a multiple of $\\theta$ if $C \\ge \\theta$. However, the actual range of $\\phi = \\angle APC$ as $P$ moves along $AB$ is $(B, 180^\\circ - A)$. While the length of this interval is indeed $C$, the solution fails to rigorously prove that such a $\\phi = k\\theta$ always exists for any initial triangle $T$ when $C < \\theta$. Specifically, if $C < \\theta$, the interval $(B, 180^\\circ - A)$ may not contain any multiple of $\\theta$.\n\nReviewer errors:\n1. OPC's claim that the inductive step is \"not necessarily true\" is discarded; splitting a vertex angle into $\\theta$ and $(k-1)\\theta$ is always possible by moving $P$ along the opposite side.\n2. OPC's claim that $\\theta=60^\\circ$ is not fully captured is discarded; the inductive logic covers it.\n3. GPT-OSS's claim that $\\theta=90^\\circ$ is a losing value for the triangle $30^\\circ, 30^\\circ, 120^\\circ$ is discarded. Mulan can cut along the altitude from an acute vertex to the opposite side, creating two right triangles, thus winning immediately.\n\nThe retained issue (the gap in the interval argument for $C < \\theta$) is a local slip/incomplete argument rather than a fallacy, as the core strategy (targeting multiples of $\\theta$) is the correct mathematical approach for this problem. The solution correctly identifies the final answer.\n\nActionable repair guidance: To complete the proof, demonstrate that if $C < \\theta$, Mulan can instead cut from a different vertex (where the angle is $\\ge \\theta$) or show that the condition $180^\\circ/\\theta = n$ allows her to eventually force an angle in $S$ regardless of the initial triangle's dimensions.\n\nFINAL_GRADE: 6/7",
